WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:05.640
A DFIR toolkit is an operational readiness system, not a random folder of utilities.

00:00:05.860 --> 00:00:13.220
It combines software, scripts, procedures, storage, templates, documentation, and team knowledge into a practical capability.

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:22.720
The goal is to help responders collect, preserve, analyze, document, and protect evidence while supporting faster and safer incident response decisions.

00:00:22.940 --> 00:00:25.260
Strong toolkits begin with requirements.

00:00:25.480 --> 00:00:38.760
They reflect the organization's investigation types, operating systems, cloud and SaaS services, endpoint coverage, network evidence needs, malware triage needs, legal constraints, policies, and team skill level.

00:00:38.980 --> 00:00:42.740
A toolkit should fit the environment and the people who will use it.

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:48.640
The best tool on paper is not the best tool during an incident if the team cannot operate it reliably.

00:00:48.860 --> 00:00:56.940
Collection, preservation, analysis, documentation, storage, automation, and maintenance all matter.

00:00:57.160 --> 00:01:00.200
Evidence collection should minimize unnecessary changes.

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:02.900
Analysis tools should be chosen by task.

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:09.840
Case notes, inventories, timelines, and decision logs make the work repeatable and defensible.

00:01:10.060 --> 00:01:13.500
Storage and transport should protect sensitive evidence.

00:01:13.720 --> 00:01:17.640
Automation should be tested, logged, and safe by default.

00:01:17.860 --> 00:01:19.780
The larger purpose is readiness.

00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:28.960
A maintained DFIR toolkit reduces delay, protects evidence, improves communication, and gives the organization better facts when pressure is high.

00:01:29.180 --> 00:01:33.020
It does not replace judgment, training, or incident response planning.

00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:34.520
It supports them.

00:01:34.740 --> 00:01:41.380
When the toolkit is prepared before the incident, the team can spend more time solving the problem and less time searching for the basics.
